(window.LADDER_CARDS["34a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["34a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"34a1.1","q":"Which four conditions make d a distance on a set X, and what does the back-triangle technique say?","a":"d(x, y) is never negative; it is 0 exactly when x = y; d(x, y) = d(y, x); and a detour never wins, d(x, z) ≤ d(x, y) + d(y, z); the distances from two points to a third differ by at most the distance between the two","w":["d is never negative; d(x, x) = 0; d(x, y) = d(y, x); and d(x, z) = d(x, y) + d(y, z); the distances to a third point always agree","d is positive; d(x, y) = −d(y, x); a detour always wins; the distances to a third point differ by at least d(x, y)","d is never negative; it is 0 exactly when x = y; a detour never wins; nothing is asked about order, so d(x, y) and d(y, x) may differ"],"why":"|d(x, z) − d(y, z)| ≤ d(x, y)."},
 {"u":"34a2.1","q":"What is the ball B(x, r), when is a part of X open, and what do the ball-open and open-family techniques say?","a":"B(x, r) is the set of y with d(x, y) < r; U is open when every point of U has some ball about it inside U; every ball is open; and ∅ and X are open, every union of open sets is open, and the overlap of two open sets is open","w":["B(x, r) is the set of y with d(x, y) ≤ r; U is open when it holds a ball; every overlap of open sets, finite or endless, is open","B(x, r) is the set of y with d(x, y) < r; U is open when some point of U has a ball inside U; unions of two open sets are open, larger unions need not be","B(x, r) is the set of y with d(x, y) = r; U is open when its leftover is empty; only ∅ and X are open"],"why":"From every point there is room to move a little and stay inside."},
 {"u":"34a3.1","q":"When does a list settle at x, when is a part closed, and what does the keeps-limits technique say?","a":"the list settles at x when d(xₙ, x) heads to 0; A is closed when its leftover X ∖ A is open; A is closed exactly when every list of its members that settles in the space settles at one of its members","w":["the list settles at x when d(xₙ, xₘ) heads to 0; A is closed when it is not open; a closed part holds every list","the list settles at x when some xₙ equals x; A is closed when its leftover is closed; a closed part has no settling lists","the list settles at x when d(xₙ, x) heads to 0; A is closed when it is not open; then [0, 1) is closed, since it is not open"],"why":"[0, 1) is neither: 1 − 1/n settles at 1, outside it."},
 {"u":"34a4.1","q":"What is the pull-back f⁻¹(V), how does it differ from 9d’s f⁻¹, and what does the pull-back technique say and not mention?","a":"the set of x in X with f(x) in V, which exists for every f, while 9d’s backwards function exists only for a bijection; a map between spaces with distances is continuous at every point exactly when the pull-back of every open set is open, a statement that speaks of open sets alone and never of the distance","w":["the function that runs f backwards, so f must be a bijection; f is continuous exactly when it sends open sets to open sets","the set of x with f(x) in V; f is continuous exactly when the pull-back of every closed ball is a ball; the statement needs the distance throughout","the set f(V); f is continuous exactly when every pull-back is not empty"],"why":"Two distances with the same open sets give the same continuous maps."},
 {"u":"34a5.1","q":"When does a list crowd, when is a space complete, and what does the closed-part technique say?","a":"a list crowds when for every ε > 0 its terms from some N on are within ε of each other; a space is complete when every list that crowds in it settles in it; with the same distance, a closed part of a complete space is complete, and a complete part of any space is closed","w":["a list crowds when it settles; every space is complete; every part of a complete space is complete","a list crowds when d(xₙ, x₁) is bounded; a space is complete when it is bounded; a closed part of any space is complete","a list crowds when its terms from some N on are within ε of each other; a space is complete when some crowding list settles; an open part of a complete space is complete"],"why":"[0, 1] is complete and (0, 1) is not: 1/2, 1/3, … leaves for 0."},
 {"u":"34a6.1","q":"Why is “each (−1/n, 1/n) is open, so their overlap is open” a move to watch out for?","a":"the open-family technique promises the overlap of finitely many open sets; these endlessly many overlap in {0} alone, and no ball about 0 fits inside {0}","w":["the overlap is empty, and ∅ is not open","each (−1/n, 1/n) is closed, not open","the overlap is (−1, 1), which is closed"],"why":"The shrinking minimum of the reaches has no positive floor."});
